(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-prevention-screening-quiz"]={"title":"Prevention and Screening Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Serious bleeding occurs in 3.0 percent of treated patients and 1.0 percent of control patients. What is the number needed to harm?","choices":["50 patients","33 patients","100 patients","2 patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The absolute increase in risk is 3.0 minus 1.0, or 0.02, and one divided by 0.02 is 50. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is overdiagnosis?","choices":["Diagnosing the same disease twice","Diagnosing disease at an earlier stage","Ordering more tests than are affordable","Finding disease that does not help the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The finding brings no benefit to the patient, so the label and any treatment can only add harm. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which practical features should a screening test have?","choices":["Invasive but highly informative","Simple, safe and inexpensive","Costly to guarantee quality","Complex and specialist-run"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A test applied to many healthy people must be simple to give, safe and cheap. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A program urges smokers to stop. Which objective does it serve?","choices":["Find disease so treatment can cure it","Find disease so treatment prevents complications","Change a behavioral risk factor","Change a metabolic risk factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Trying to get people to stop smoking is the standard example of targeting a behavioral risk factor in order to change the behavior. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the negative labeling effect of screening?","choices":["Harm from being labeled as having a condition","A specimen recorded under the wrong name","Patients labeling themselves noncompliant","A result filed under the wrong diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is an ethical concern, since telling someone they have a condition can harm them whether or not the label helps. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which condition is suited to a community screening program?","choices":["A rare disease with no effective treatment","A mild disease found in everyone","A serious problem with effective treatment","A disease whose course is poorly understood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It should be an important public health problem, with effective treatment available and a well-understood natural history. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A test with sensitivity 95 percent and specificity 98 percent screens 20,000 people, of whom 0.5 percent have the disease. What is the positive predictive value?","choices":["About 19 percent","About 95 percent","About 98 percent","About 81 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There are 95 true positives (95 of 100) and 398 false positives (2 percent of 19,900), so 95 divided by 493 is about 19 percent. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which objective fits a program that screens for sexually transmitted infections?","choices":["Find disease so treatment prevents complications","Find disease so treatment can cure it","Change a behavioral risk factor","Change a metabolic risk factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sexually transmitted infections and cancers are examples of diseases screened for so that treatment can cure patients. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"On the timeline of disease, where does secondary prevention act?","choices":["Before any disease exists","While disease is asymptomatic","Throughout the clinical course","Only at the moment of onset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The timeline runs from no disease, to asymptomatic disease, to the clinical course, and early detection belongs to the middle phase. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Ideally, what pairing of the two numbers is desired for an intervention?","choices":["High number needed to treat, low number needed to harm","Both numbers low","Both numbers high","Low number needed to treat, high number needed to harm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Few patients need treatment for one to benefit, and many must be treated before one is harmed. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A test with sensitivity 90 percent and specificity 95 percent is used to screen 10,000 people, of whom 1 percent have the disease. How many false-positive results are expected?","choices":["90 people","500 people","495 people","10 people"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 9,900 people are healthy and 5 percent of them test positive, giving 495; only 90 of the 100 with disease test positive. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What evidence should exist before screening is introduced?","choices":["That screened people live longer after diagnosis","That screening alters the natural history of disease","That the test detects any abnormality","That most people would accept screening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classic statement asks for conclusive evidence that screening changes the natural history in a significant proportion of those screened. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A treatment halves the risk of an event. In a group whose baseline risk is 2 percent, what is the number needed to treat?","choices":["100 patients","50 patients","2 patients","200 patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Halving 2 percent leaves 1 percent, an absolute reduction of 0.01, and one divided by 0.01 is 100. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What sensitivity should a screening test have?","choices":["High sensitivity early in the disease","High sensitivity only late in disease","Low sensitivity to avoid overdiagnosis","Sensitivity does not matter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A test that does not miss disease in its early stage is what makes early detection possible. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Overdiagnosis is an extreme example of which screening bias?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Compliance bias","Recall bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Screening picks up the slowest conditions, and at the extreme they are so indolent that finding them does not help the patient. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Preventive care interventions are offered to which group of people?","choices":["People who present with new symptoms","People who are severely ill","People seeking a second opinion","People without complaints"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is defined as interventions for people without complaints, to modify risk factors or to find disease early in its course. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which measures must be known about a screening test before a program uses it?","choices":["Sensitivity and specificity, without predictive values","Predictive values, without sensitivity or specificity","Sensitivity, specificity and predictive values","Cost and speed, without accuracy measures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These four measures must be known and be acceptable before the test is used in a program. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does a large number needed to treat mean?","choices":["Many must be treated for one to benefit","Most treated patients benefit","The intervention is very safe","The intervention causes frequent harm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it suggests a small absolute benefit per patient treated, whereas a small number needed to treat means the intervention is more effective. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A trial has 500 patients per arm. The control arm has 30 events and the treated arm has 20. What is the number needed to treat?","choices":["50 patients","10 patients","25 patients","17 patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Risk falls from 6 percent (30 of 500) to 4 percent (20 of 500), a reduction of 0.02, and one divided by 0.02 is 50. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Over five years, 40 of 1,000 untreated patients and 25 of 1,000 treated patients have a fracture. What is the number needed to treat?","choices":["15 patients","25 patients","40 patients","About 67 patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk falls from 4.0 percent to 2.5 percent, an absolute reduction of 0.015, and one divided by 0.015 is about 67. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does primary prevention do?","choices":["Detects disease that is still asymptomatic","Reduces complications after diagnosis","Confirms disease in symptomatic patients","Removes causes or risk factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It prevents disease from occurring, and it can be applied to an individual or to a whole community. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A program identifies people with high cholesterol so that they can change their diet. What does it target?","choices":["A behavioral risk factor","A curable infection","A complication of established disease","A metabolic risk factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cholesterol is a metabolic risk factor, and modifying diet to lower it is intended to avoid coronary artery disease. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A program finds mostly slow-growing cancers, and screened patients appear to outlive unscreened patients whose cancers were found by symptoms. Which bias best explains this?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Compliance bias","Attrition bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Screening catches more slow, favorable conditions, so the screened group looks better even if screening changed nothing. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"When are observational studies used to study primary prevention?","choices":["When a randomized trial would be faster","When bias needs to be eliminated","When randomization is not possible or ethical","When the intervention is a vaccine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Randomized trials are preferred, and observational designs fill the gap where people cannot be randomly assigned. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Two treatments prevent the same outcome over the same period. Treatment X has a number needed to treat of 25 and Treatment Y has 60. Which conclusion follows?","choices":["Treatment X is more effective","Treatment Y is more effective","They are equally effective","Treatment X is more harmful"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A smaller number needed to treat means fewer patients must be treated for one to benefit, so X does more per patient. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the four types of clinical prevention?","choices":["Palliative care","Emergency stabilization","Chemoprevention","Surgical resection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The four types are immunization, screening, behavioral or lifestyle counseling, and chemoprevention, all of which are built into routine practice. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the number needed to harm measure?","choices":["Patients treated for one to benefit","Patients receiving an intervention for one to be harmed","Patients screened for one false-positive result","Patients followed for one to be lost"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It counts how many patients must have a given intervention for one of them to experience a harm. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which objective fits a program that screens for hypertension?","choices":["Find disease so treatment can cure it","Find disease so treatment prevents complications","Change a behavioral risk factor","Change a metabolic risk factor through diet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treating hypertension prevents its complications, unlike screening for conditions where treatment can cure. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"An intervention has a number needed to treat of 25 and a number needed to harm of 20 for an equally serious outcome. What does this show?","choices":["Benefit is more likely than harm","Benefit and harm are equally likely","Harm is more likely than benefit","The pairing is ideal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One patient in 20 is harmed and only one in 25 benefits, so harm outweighs benefit in frequency. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Volunteers who attend screening have lower death rates than people who decline, though screening itself offers no benefit. Which bias is this?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Overdiagnosis","Compliance bias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. People who attend tend to be more invested in their health, and that alone can lower their death rates. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
